---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import FAQList from '../components/FAQList.astro';
import PlanningCTA from '../components/PlanningCTA.astro';
import { images } from '../data/assets';
import { groupTravelPages } from '../data/landing';
const breadcrumbs=[{label:'Private Group Travel',href:'/private-group-travel/'}];
const faqs=[
  {q:'What is private group travel?',a:'Private group travel is a trip reserved or designed for a specific group rather than individual travelers joining a general public departure. The itinerary, pace, rooming, activities, support, and commercial structure can be shaped around that group.'},
  {q:'How many travelers do we need?',a:'There is no single minimum that applies to every trip. The practical range depends on destination, accommodation, transport, activities, and how fixed costs are shared. We can begin with an estimated range rather than a final number.'},
  {q:'Can we create a trip around our own dates?',a:'Yes, subject to supplier availability, season, local operating conditions, and any permit or event constraints.'},
  {q:'Can Talon help the organizer present the trip to members?',a:'Yes. Organizer support can include helping structure the information participants need to understand the itinerary, inclusions, activity level, likely cost, deadlines, and preparation requirements.'},
  {q:'Can travelers arrive from different cities?',a:'Often. The trip can use coordinated flight planning, a common arrival window, or a destination meeting point depending on the group and itinerary.'},
  {q:'Can the group include different activity levels?',a:'Sometimes. Optional routes, split activities, rest choices, or alternate experiences can help, but every trip still needs a clear minimum participation standard.'}
];
const serviceSchema={'@type':'Service',name:'Private Group Travel',description:'Custom private group travel planning for clubs, communities, businesses, studios, teams, friends, and affinity groups.',provider:{'@id':`${Astro.site}#organization`},areaServed:{'@type':'Place',name:'Worldwide'}};
const faqSchema={'@type':'FAQPage',mainEntity:faqs.map((i)=>({'@type':'Question',name:i.q,acceptedAnswer:{'@type':'Answer',text:i.a}}))};
---
<BaseLayout title="Private Group Travel Planning | Talon Expeditions" description="Custom private group travel planning for clubs, communities, businesses, studios, teams, friends, and affinity groups, with itinerary design, organizer support, and worldwide coordination." image={images.group} breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} schema={[serviceSchema,faqSchema]}>
  <section class="page-hero" style={`background-image:url('${images.group}')`}><div class="shell"><Breadcrumbs items={breadcrumbs}/><p class="eyebrow">Private group travel</p><h1 class="section-title">Build the trip around your people instead of asking your people to fit a generic departure.</h1><p>Private group travel is the center of the Talon Expeditions model. We help clubs, communities, businesses, studios, teams, friends, and organizers create trips around their own purpose, dates, activity level, comfort, and group dynamics.</p></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell definition-grid"><div class="definition-sticky"><p class="eyebrow">What it is</p><h2>Custom travel for a group that already has a reason to be together.</h2></div><div class="definition-body"><div class="answer-panel"><h2>How does private group travel with Talon work?</h2><p>We begin with the group rather than a fixed tour. Together we define the travelers, purpose, timing, likely size, activity level, comfort, and approximate budget. Talon then develops the itinerary and coordinates the travel structure, local suppliers, participant information, readiness, and organizer support required to move the trip toward departure.</p></div><p>A private group may be highly experienced and know exactly what it wants to do. Another may have only a community and a broad idea such as “a hiking trip next fall” or “a warm-weather learn-to-dive week.” Both are valid starting points. The important thing is to establish the real constraints before expensive commitments are made.</p><p>Private does not necessarily mean luxury, guided every day, or expensive. It means the trip is organized for one group rather than built around strangers joining a fixed calendar. The support level can be light or comprehensive depending on the destination and how much work the organizer wants to carry.</p></div></div></section>

  <section class="section soft-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">What Talon can handle</p><h2 class="section-title">The work between “we should do a trip” and “we are ready to leave.”</h2></div><p class="section-copy">The exact scope varies by itinerary. These are the major areas that can be brought into one planning relationship.</p></div><div class="donor-grid"><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">01</span><h3>Trip concept & destination</h3><p>Clarify purpose, compare destinations, evaluate season, choose the travel style, and decide what the trip needs to protect.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">02</span><h3>Itinerary & pacing</h3><p>Build the route, activity mix, rest, cultural time, transfers, free time, and daily rhythm around the group.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">03</span><h3>Accommodation & transport</h3><p>Coordinate rooming assumptions, hotels or lodges, private or shared ground transport, rail, ferries, and arrival logistics.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">04</span><h3>Activities & specialists</h3><p>Work with appropriate local operators, guides, instructors, cruise suppliers, activity providers, and destination services.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">05</span><h3>Organizer support</h3><p>Help structure the trip information, participant questions, launch sequence, group-size assumptions, and decision milestones.</p></div><div class="donor-card"><span class="card-index">06</span><h3>Traveler readiness</h3><p>Clarify documents, insurance, rooming, fitness, equipment, certifications, arrival details, and the final travel picture.</p></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="section tabbed-feature"><div class="shell tabbed-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Groups we plan for</p><h2 class="section-title">The itinerary should change when the community changes.</h2><ul class="category-menu"><li class="active">Clubs & outdoor communities</li><li>Dive & watersports groups</li><li>Wellness & studio communities</li><li>Businesses & teams</li><li>Social & affinity groups</li><li>Friends & private groups</li></ul></div><div class="category-feature" style={`background-image:url('${images.wildlife}')`}><div class="feature-note"><h3>Group-first is an operating choice.</h3><p>A hiking club, yoga studio, company team, and alumni group may all be “groups,” but their activity tolerance, rooming, communication, social expectations, and reasons for traveling are different. We plan around that difference.</p></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Explore by organizer type</p><h2 class="section-title">Start with the community you are responsible for.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">Each audience page goes deeper into the planning questions that are specific to that kind of group.</p></div><div class="category-card-grid">{groupTravelPages.map((page)=><a class="category-card" href={`/group-travel/${page.slug}/`}><div class="category-card-image" style={`background-image:url('${page.hero}')`}></div><div class="category-card-body"><h3>{page.title}</h3><p>{page.description}</p><span>See the planning model →</span></div></a>)}</div></div></section>

  <section class="section dark-section"><div class="shell donor-detail"><div><div class="detail-gallery"><div class="gallery-main" style={`background-image:url('${images.trekking}')`}></div><div style={`background-image:url('${images.coast}')`}></div><div style={`background-image:url('${images.city}')`}></div></div></div><div><p class="eyebrow">Custom, but disciplined</p><h2 class="section-title">A private trip gets better when the group is honest about its priorities.</h2><p style="color:#aaa">Customization creates choices. It does not remove tradeoffs. More destinations usually mean more transfers. Higher activity can narrow the group. More private services can raise cost. More free time may reduce how much can be prearranged.</p><div class="detail-body" style="grid-template-columns:1fr"><div class="trip-facts" style="background:#171310"><h3 style="color:#fff">Early decisions that matter</h3><div class="fact"><strong>Purpose</strong><span style="color:#aaa">Why are these people traveling together?</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Group size</strong><span style="color:#aaa">Minimum, target, and realistic maximum.</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Activity</strong><span style="color:#aaa">What level can the actual participants manage?</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Comfort</strong><span style="color:#aaa">Where should the trip sit between simple and premium?</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Budget</strong><span style="color:#aaa">What is realistic when major extras are considered?</span></div><div class="fact"><strong>Support</strong><span style="color:#aaa">What does the organizer want Talon to own?</span></div></div></div></div></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="shell process-donor"><div class="process-visual" style={`background-image:url('${images.planning}')`}><div class="process-callout"><h3>You do not need a finished itinerary.</h3><p>A broad idea and a realistic description of the group are enough to start.</p></div></div><div><p class="eyebrow">How it works</p><h2 class="section-title">A planning process designed around commitment, not endless options.</h2><p class="section-copy">The early stages stay flexible while we test fit. The later stages become more specific as suppliers, travelers, payments, and operating details are confirmed.</p><div class="process-list"><div class="process-item"><b>Define the Trip</b><p>Group, purpose, dates, experience, activity, comfort, budget, and constraints.</p></div><div class="process-item"><b>Shape the Plan</b><p>Destination, route, lodging, transport, suppliers, support, and likely commercial structure.</p></div><div class="process-item"><b>Prepare to Launch</b><p>Participant information, activity expectations, inclusions, price assumptions, deadlines, and decision path.</p></div><div class="process-item"><b>Prepare to Travel</b><p>Confirmed travelers, documents, rooming, equipment, flights, final itinerary, and support contacts.</p></div></div><a class="button" href="/how-it-works/">See the Full Planning Process</a></div></div></section>

  <section class="section soft-section"><div class="shell"><div class="specialty-heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Questions organizers ask</p><h2 class="section-title">The earlier these questions are answered, the easier the trip is to run.</h2></div><p class="section-copy">Specific supplier and booking terms are confirmed on the actual proposal. These answers explain the general private-group model.</p></div><FAQList items={faqs} openFirst={true}/><div style="margin-top:28px"><a class="text-arrow" href="/faq/private-group-travel/">Read the full Private Group Travel FAQ →</a></div></div></section>

  <section class="quote-strip"><div class="shell"><strong>The organizer should lead the community, not become its unpaid travel operations department.</strong><p>Talon’s role is to reduce fragmented coordination and make the plan easier for both the leader and the travelers to understand.</p></div></section>
  <PlanningCTA title="Have the group but not the whole plan? That is a normal place to begin." />
</BaseLayout>
